  {"type": "split", "photo": "in", "reverse": False, "h2": "Moving an old farmhouse off propane or oil",
   "html": "<p>An old farmhouse is a different animal from a new subdivision home. Thick plaster walls, additions from three different decades, and a stone or block foundation that was never meant to hold a furnace. Many of them have heated with oil or propane for as long as anyone remembers.</p>"
           "<p>A cold-climate heat pump can take over most of that heating. The key is sizing. Too big and it short cycles in spring and fall. Too small and it leans on backup heat every cold night. We run a Manual J heat-loss calculation room by room, look at insulation and windows, and size for the colder, snowier weather up on the Oak Ridges Moraine.</p>"
           "<p>If the house has ducts, a ducted heat pump with a Midea electric air handler can replace the oil or propane furnace outright. If it has baseboards or no ducts at all, a ductless or multi-zone system is usually the cleaner route.</p>"
           "<ul class=\"checklist\"><li>Heat-loss sizing based on your actual walls, windows and insulation</li><li>Ducted, ductless or multi-zone, matched to how the house is built</li><li>Electric backup heat or a kept furnace for the deepest cold</li><li>ESA-licensed electrical work for the 240V circuit and any panel upgrade</li></ul>"},
